so Greta tell us about your writing  process for this movie well it took me  it took me a very long time to write it  I tend to take a long time to write  because I wish I didn't but that's just  kind of how it goes I I started with  wanting to make something about home and  how home is something you only can  really understand as you're leaving and  I knew I wanted the core of it to be a  love story between a mother and a  daughter and I don't I start with a  hunch and a kind of general sense of it  but I don't I don't know what its gonna  be until I start making it and and I  think I spent a lot of time letting the  characters talk to me and tell me who  they are and then I I wind up with a  very long draft which I then have to cut  down and that's what I did on this one  and then I you know I I don't write with  actors in mind but if I had I I would  there's no better actors I could have  possibly thought of to to have to work  with so Saoirse and Greta sort of  connected at the Toronto Film Festival  in 2015 you both happen to be there and  you had to kind of read through I guess  and and can you talk a little bit about  what happened and why you became  involved in the film yeah and I mean I  became involved or wanted to be involved  instantly as soon as I knew it was Greta  script and she's someone who I've always  admired and always been a very big fan  of and she knows that um and we we I  read it and of course I loved it because  look at how great it was and AM and we  did a Skype meeting which was the giddy  escape meeting I've ever had was not  professional at all it was like two best  friends that hadn't seen each other all  summer and had finally come back  together and and then we met in Toronto  and I remember I had just flown in to do  press for Brooklyn and Greta had flown  in from it  that she had been shooting on and we  went through the whole script and it  just felt like you had kind of met your  person a little bit felt like you met  the person that you just want to make  everything with you know and which means  you have to put me in every single one  of your films now with her but but yeah  and that and that was it really we  clicked and then a year later we we got  to make it but it was lovely also after  Greta allowed me to come on board that  we had the year to sort of stay in touch  and talk about things and meet up in New  York and it was good to have that time  to let it breathe a little bit you know  Laurie you and Tracy had sort of  travelled in the same circles in theatre  but you had never worked together before  is that right yeah that's right and so  what was it like kind of creating a  family on set did you guys do some  rehearsals or how did you sort of find  that Saoirse and I got to rehearse and I  hear that euro said oh you'd never got  the rear I didn't get any damn role but  I knew that Tracy and I would be on the  same page right away we just share the  same  I don't know work style I guess and  sense of humor and and everything was on  the page everything was scripted by  Greta so it was really easy to click  into the this this family dynamic Tracy  as a writer yourself what was your  experience reading the script what did  you make of it it was it's what you see  up there the the movie in my mind when I  read it is the movie that Greta made  that's not always the case the script  was very tight I don't remember it going  through a lot of changes between the  time I read it at the time we shot it I  don't recall it going through a lot of  changes on the set I mean she was open  to change stuff if we needed to but for  the most part it was all there's  everything you need to play the role  which makes our job just that much  easier John the music evokes a very  specific moment in time can you tell us  what your conversations were with Greta  about the music for this film well it's  supposed to be just that  that moment and somebody's life you know  you sense they're supposed to be more  but you can't articulate it yet and you  know we all understand that one in fact  it's a point you can find yourself and  multiple times in your life so there's a  certain sort of bittersweet thing to  that yearning but it's nonspecific so it  was more about chasing something that  echoed that feeling and usually for us  and we were sitting around working when  it was right we knew it and we both sort  of point at the same time and then it  was done  Saoirse is there a difference when  you're acting for someone who's also an  actor I think there can be yeah I mean  in this case there definitely was  because well yeah actually any time I  worked was a director who's also an  actor there's a sensitivity there and an  understanding for what we do and  regardless of how an actor works the  sort of process I suppose an actor's  mind goes through when they're creating  a role and all that and you know even if  you're kind of you know you have a thick  enough skin I think it's still it's a  vulnerable thing to do and so to have  someone who was so sensitive to that  really helped because Greta could be  strong for us when we needed someone to  be strong and she could allow us to take  the lead when we felt we could do it on  her own I mean it honestly felt like she  was our mother for the whole thing it  did and I think that's what a great  director does it's like the equivalent  to a great parent somebody who like you  know gives you that discipline and that  structure but also gives you like the  love and the freedom to feel like you  can do anything Greta you had done a lot  of different jobs on on films you you've  written you've co-directed you've acted  and you've been in many independent  films where you have to sort of help out  with all the gigs on on a set how did  you decide that this was the movie where  you were going to take on directing solo  well I thought I've always wanted to be  a director I've always specifically  be a writer-director as something that I  wrote and and like you were saying at  all these things I've done and I didn't  go to film school so so this is with all  this time that I've spent on sets doing  different jobs has been my film school  and it's an extraordinary way to learn  because I've been able to be around so  many great directors and  cinematographers and sound mixers and  production designers and costume  designers and I've gotten to see how  different people run things and and  figure it out and when I finished this  script I knew I knew that if I didn't do  it now I would not I would never do it  and there's always more to learn but I  think I'd reached the point where the  learning would happen by doing I wasn't  going to keep learning on the sidelines  anymore and and I just decided to go to  jump there are a lot of great  coming-of-age movies that capture a sort  of boy at the age that this movie  depicts not as many that capture a girl  at this age what was why was this  particular age of interest to you well  you know I think it's what John just  said so beautifully about the yearning  for something you don't quite know what  it is so there's that that is so vivid I  think particularly at 17 and for me I I  wanted to make a film where that the  primary question of this for this young  woman was was that of her coming into  her own person hood and what does that  mean to occupy that space and I think  often young female characters are given  validation through being chosen as a  romantic partner and she has her  romantic partners but I kind of wanted  to start from this premise of you know  what if there are two guys and they're  both wrong and the love stories with her  mother and that felt more true to life  to me and I felt like a way to  explore who's stepping into who she  already is in a way and and I that  that's the kind of story I'm interested  in thank you very much
 

